+`--spec-min-p 0.7` was slightly ahead in all three rounds. Settings are exhausted: the rest is engine work (the PLE
+reads, the CPU experts, the per-layer GPU latency).
+
+## The PLE table in a file of its own (2026-10-03)
+
+Layer 1's n-gram (PLE) table is read from the SSD while decoding: 16 rows of 90 B per token, before every verify
+window. In Unsloth's layout the 28.8 GB table shares shard 2 with 296 other tensors, and the engine keeps a mapping of
+that whole shard (experts and dense weights in place). On Windows, unbuffered reads of a file that also has a live
+mapping take a much slower path. The engine logged 4-6 ms per read (p50), where the drive answers in ~0.2 ms; ISTA's
+original layout had the table alone in its shard, which no one maps, so upstream never met this.
+
+`tools/v100_ple_split.py` (`v100\13_ple_split.bat`, no GPU) measured it with the engine's own reader
+(`ple_reader_test --map-only`, new): 16 rows per token every 40 ms took 0.78 ms with nothing mapped and 2.43 ms with
+the shard mapped. It then copies the table byte for byte into a one-tensor GGUF next to the shards
+(`*-ple-table.gguf`, architecture `strata-ple`, the form `--ple-gguf` already accepts), checks 4,002 rows, and sets
+`--ple-gguf` in the config (`v100_setup.py` keeps it; `--undo` reverts). From its own file: 0.55 ms per token.
+
+`v100_decodebench7.log` (config with the table file, vs `--ple-gguf` removed; each twice, alternating):
+
+| | Short chats | After 60K | 60K prompt | PLE wait per window | PLE read p50 |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| table from the shard (before) | 57.3 / 56.2 | 47.5 / 51.0 | 85 s | 4-8 ms | 3.6-6.5 ms |
+| **table from its own file** | **56.8 / 59.8** | **55.0 / 52.6** | **67 s** | **0.1-1.1 ms** | **0.2-0.8 ms** |
+
+Same rows, same answers. Writing is ~5-10% faster (first runs of each chat +8-14%, when the rows are not cached
+yet), and reading a long prompt ~20% faster (~880 vs ~700 tok/s), since the prompt path reads the same table.
+
+## Your own workload: short reads, and the expert caches (2026-10-03)
+
+`v100\15_workload.bat` (`tools/v100_workload.py`) runs the server for real work with the timing switches on and
+records the decode routing (`runs\workload-*\routing.bin`, no text). The first hour (152 requests, up to 213K
+context) showed most prompt time going to small new messages read through the batched path (65-768 new tokens:
+3.6-10 s each - the V100 streams every routed expert it lacks over its x4 link); the decode windows read ~12 ms a
+token. `--short-read 768` (was 64; `v100_setup.py` sets it) made those 2-3x faster in the second run (76-130 tokens
+0.9-1.7 s, 489 tokens 5.4 s); batched stays faster above ~1,000 tokens. `"fit_max_tokens": true` lets a client ask
+for "the rest of the context" from its own (low) prompt estimate without a 400.
+
+`tools/v100_cachesim.py` (no GPU) replays that routing through a model of the caches and the adaptive tier (within
+~1.5 points of the engine's logged hit rate). The 4070 Super's cache holds 17% of its layers' experts and answers
+~55% of their lookups; the V100's holds 58% and answers ~94%, so ~85% of the CPU's expert work is the 4070 Super's
+layers. An expert profile built from the routing helped only the run it was made from (held out: -0.2 points): the
+adaptive tier already follows the conversation, and swaps stay within a layer. What does help: the shared budget of
+32 swaps per round left the 4070 Super ~17; `--adapt-stage-swaps 96,32` (new: a budget per card, CUDA0 first)
+replayed 77.0 -> 80.0%, and each extra GiB for CUDA0's cache is worth ~2 points. `v100\16_expert_cache.bat`
+benches the per-card budgets on the GPUs (`v100_decodebench8.log`).
+
+`v100_decodebench8.log` (decode windows after each variant's warm-up, summed):
+
+| | GPU hits | ms/window | CPU experts ms | 4070S pool+plan | V100 wait for GPU | tok/s |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| shared 32 (config) | 73.5% | 40.6 | 13.1 | 10.8 | 14.1 | 57.8 |
+| 64,32 | 76.1% | 41.2 | 11.9 | 10.2 | 15.4 | 57.0 |
+| 96,32 | 76.4% | 40.6 | 11.7 | 9.9 | 15.0 | 57.3 |
+| 160,32 | 77.7% | 40.2 | 11.3 | 9.4 | 14.9 | 57.0 |
+| 96,16 | 74.7% | 40.1 | 12.3 | 9.7 | 13.9 | 57.4 |
+| shared 32 again | 73.4% | 39.5 | 12.7 | 10.6 | 13.9 | 57.5 |
+
+The replay was right about the hits (+4 points at 160,32) and the 4070 Super's CPU time fell ~1.4 ms a window, but
+the windows did not get shorter beyond the run-to-run drift (~1 ms): the V100's stage (~14-15 ms waiting for its
+GPU) took up the difference. The config keeps `--adapt-swaps 32`; the option stays for later. The CPU misses of
+the 4070 Super's layers are not what sets the window time now.
+
